1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Four civilisations counted —
the Moon’s rooms.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Tonight’s Moon is staying in a room with
a name.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
“The 28 mansions are lodges of the Sun
and Moon.” — Wang Chong, 1st century CE

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The Moon takes 27.3 days to circle the
sky.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
China cut that path into 28 mansions,
starting from Jiao (the Horns).

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Draw the borders: the biggest, Jing (the
Well), spans 33°. The Moon needs 2.5
days.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The smallest, Zui (the Turtle Beak), is
just 2° — crossed in 3.5 hours.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
So China’s mansions were never built for
“one night, one room.”

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
India cut the ecliptic into 27 equal
parts, each exactly 13°20′.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
There was once a 28th, Abhijit, by Vega.
Splitting evenly pushed it out.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The Arabs called them manāzil — the
Moon’s stations. Twenty-eight of them.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
“Four civilisations each invented the 28
mansions” — that doesn’t hold up.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Japan and Korea adopted China’s; the
Arab system most likely came from India.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
China or India first, or each on its
own? Scholars still argue.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The Great Square of Pegasus stands like
a diamond. Its right corner is Shi (the
Room).

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Next Wednesday: another people, another
sky.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

